{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0194n7zYpjaMwhzXewPvCcMp","type":"tool_result","content":"47:Eight questions. None of them requires this chapter — every one is answerable from a chapter you have already finished. Your score tells you how to read what follows.\n76:5. **Because container logs are written to the node's filesystem**, by the container runtime, for every container the node runs. Collecting them is a per-node job, and the workload resource that puts exactly one Pod on every node is the DaemonSet. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §7 — one per node, and work that ends]*\n234:OpenTelemetry — the CNCF project that defines the vendor-neutral standard for telemetry data — currently supports four signals [source: opentelemetry-signals-2026-08-23]:\n400:**4.** *[retrieval: ch5]* A Pod's containers declare `limits: cpu: 1000m` but declare no `requests` at all. The node has 8 CPUs. An HPA is configured to target 70% CPU utilization for this Deployment. What does the HPA do?\n404:- C) Takes no action on that metric, because CPU utilization is undefined\n405:- D) Defaults the request to the limit and scales on 70% of 1000m\n842:The workload resource for \"exactly one Pod on every node\" is the **DaemonSet** *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §7 — one per node, and work that ends]*, and node-level log collection is its canonical example. When a new node joins the cluster, it gets the agent automatically, because that is what a DaemonSet does.\n844:**2. A dedicated sidecar container** in the application Pod, collecting that application's logs. Useful when an application writes to a file inside the container rather than to stdout, or needs per-application processing. The cost is one extra container per Pod rather than one per node — which, on a node running forty Pods, is a meaningfully different resource bill.\n846:**3. The application pushes directly** to a logging backend from within the application. No collector at all; the app knows the backend's address and speaks its protocol. Simple, and it couples your application code to your logging vendor.\n1016:**2.** The three: **a node-level logging agent** (typically a DaemonSet), **a dedicated sidecar container** in the application Pod, and **the application pushing directly** to a backend.\n1149:Seventeen questions. Four are interleaved with earlier chapters and tagged. Explanations follow all seventeen; resist the urge to scroll.\n1198:**Q6.** *[retrieval: ch5]* A Deployment's Pod template sets CPU limits but no CPU requests. An HPA targets 60% CPU utilization for it. What happens?\n1202:- C) CPU utilization is undefined for the Pod and the HPA takes no action on that metric\n1337:- **D** is wrong: a Pod with limits and no requests is admitted normally. (A LimitRange *may* default a request at admission, which is a separate mechanism operating before the Pod exists — not the HPA improvising a denominator.)\n1380:- **A** is wrong: sub-project, not fork, and both sit under the CNCF.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7ddda16a-a6b0-4f37-b33e-4446373be055","uuid":"73c11ab3-712f-428d-a47e-48b1ebf3a58a","timestamp":"2026-08-31T21:16:27.350Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"47:Eight questions. None of them requires this chapter — every one is answerable from a chapter you have already finished. Your score tells you how to read what follows.\n76:5. **Because container logs are written to the node's filesystem**, by the container runtime, for every container the node runs. Collecting them is a per-node job, and the workload resource that puts exactly one Pod on every node is the DaemonSet. *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §7 — one per node, and work that ends]*\n234:OpenTelemetry — the CNCF project that defines the vendor-neutral standard for telemetry data — currently supports four signals [source: opentelemetry-signals-2026-08-23]:\n400:**4.** *[retrieval: ch5]* A Pod's containers declare `limits: cpu: 1000m` but declare no `requests` at all. The node has 8 CPUs. An HPA is configured to target 70% CPU utilization for this Deployment. What does the HPA do?\n404:- C) Takes no action on that metric, because CPU utilization is undefined\n405:- D) Defaults the request to the limit and scales on 70% of 1000m\n842:The workload resource for \"exactly one Pod on every node\" is the **DaemonSet** *[cross-bearing: see Ch 6 §7 — one per node, and work that ends]*, and node-level log collection is its canonical example. When a new node joins the cluster, it gets the agent automatically, because that is what a DaemonSet does.\n844:**2. A dedicated sidecar container** in the application Pod, collecting that application's logs. Useful when an application writes to a file inside the container rather than to stdout, or needs per-application processing. The cost is one extra container per Pod rather than one per node — which, on a node running forty Pods, is a meaningfully different resource bill.\n846:**3. The application pushes directly** to a logging backend from within the application. No collector at all; the app knows the backend's address and speaks its protocol. Simple, and it couples your application code to your logging vendor.\n1016:**2.** The three: **a node-level logging agent** (typically a DaemonSet), **a dedicated sidecar container** in the application Pod, and **the application pushing directly** to a backend.\n1149:Seventeen questions. Four are interleaved with earlier chapters and tagged. Explanations follow all seventeen; resist the urge to scroll.\n1198:**Q6.** *[retrieval: ch5]* A Deployment's Pod template sets CPU limits but no CPU requests. An HPA targets 60% CPU utilization for it. What happens?\n1202:- C) CPU utilization is undefined for the Pod and the HPA takes no action on that metric\n1337:- **D** is wrong: a Pod with limits and no requests is admitted normally. (A LimitRange *may* default a request at admission, which is a separate mechanism operating before the Pod exists — not the HPA improvising a denominator.)\n1380:- **A** is wrong: sub-project, not fork, and both sit under the CNCF.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
